env_0054_ecommerce_marketplace_operator is finished. All three delivery conditions hold: every difficulty passes the task-and-policy audit, every product's KB documents pass their audit, and `validate_scenario` returns `VALID` with 0 warnings. I re-ran every gate myself at the end and all are green.

**What's in `env/env_0054_ecommerce_marketplace_operator/`**
- **Blueprint:** "Northloop Marketplace" with 7 product categories (`order`, `selling`, `fulfill`, `payout`, `standing`, `member`, `protect`), 24 tables and shared tools.
- **5 difficulties, 2 tasks each:**

| Difficulty | Audit rounds (tasks / KB) |
|---|---|
| order returns, refunds & guarantee | 3 / 2 |
| seller fees & selling plans | 3 / 2 |
| FBN storage & reimbursements | 2 / 2 |
| seller payouts, reserves & chargebacks | 2 / 1 |
| seller standing & account actions | 4 / 1 |

- **KB:** 332 documents across all 7 products. `member` and `protect` belong to no difficulty, so as designed they were checked only by the document gate and never audited.
- **Gates:** all 9 per-difficulty gates pass for every difficulty, including traceability. No customer identifiers repeat across difficulties, and the frozen blueprint was not changed.

**Things worth knowing**
- **Seller standing difficulty:** on the first pass its agent wrote nothing, saying the standing product had no numbers for a seller to act on. I kept the blueprint frozen and had it work around this inside its own folder. It declared replacements for some base rules and used violation points as the computed values. It then took four audit rounds, partly because its first rules weren't realistic. The auditors still call some of its relief schedules plausible but invented.
- **Order difficulty, cross-difficulty factors:** the evaluator only lets a buyer's calculation read the buyer's own records. So the factors that are supposed to read another product read Loop+ membership, which no difficulty claims. The auditor accepted that, but under a literal reading of the rule both order tasks would fail it.
- **Seller fees, renewal rule:** the KB writer for the seller fees product found that the early-termination rule's text and its calculation disagreed. I sent it back for a fix, a new task audit, a re-render and a new KB audit. No scored value moved.
- **Seller fees, missing payments entries:** the fee credits and early termination fees book only the selling-side record. The matching payments-account entry is never written, because writing it would trigger a change in another product. Both audits passed with this recorded.
- **Not reworked:** the instant-payout fee adds up all of a day's instant payouts while the rule charges each payout separately. Each payout task has at most one instant payout per day, so no answer changes.
- **Not reworked:** a few payout rules contain words broken across a line ("payout- limit", "on- demand").
- **Blueprint wording:** the KB writers had to turn code-style names in some base rules into plain words. They and the KB audits also noted a few gaps in the base rules. None affects a task, and they are recorded in the worklog as known issues.

**Interruptions:** the run was cut off twice by account limits. Each time I checked what was actually on disk and resumed the same agents, and no finished work was redone.

The full history is in `worklog.md` in the scenario folder; audit reports are under `audits/`, and the inputs given to auditors are under `.audit_views/`. I also saved a memory note with lessons for future runs.
